---
import "@skryensya/core/components/dialog.css";
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import ComponentPreview from "../../../components/ComponentPreview.astro";
import {
  headingCompactTree,
  headingDisplayTree,
  headingDocumentTree,
  headingOutlineTree,
  headingPageTitleTree,
  headingFlushTree,
} from "../../../demos/typography";
import { getLocale, useTranslations } from "../../../i18n";

const install = `pnpm add @skryensya/core @skryensya/react`;
const t = useTranslations(getLocale(Astro.url));

---

<Base title="Heading" description="Heading: independent semantic hierarchy and visual size.">
  <h1>Heading</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Heading separates the hierarchy of the document from its appearance. Choose <code>as</code> based
    on the semantic level and <code>size</code> based on the size the visual context needs.
  </p>

  <h2>Sizes</h2>
  <p>
    Three displays above the document scale. In the document, <code>h1</code>–<code>h4</code> step
    down; <code>h5</code> and <code>h6</code> share the <code>h4</code> floor (18px).
  </p>

  <h3>Display</h3>
  <ComponentPreview tree={headingDisplayTree(t)} label="Display scale" />

  <h3>Document</h3>
  <ComponentPreview tree={headingDocumentTree(t)} label="h1–h6 scale" />

  <h2>Page title</h2>
  <p>The single <code>h1</code> presents the page; a display adds presence without inventing other semantics.</p>
  <ComponentPreview tree={headingPageTitleTree(t)} label="Page title" />

  <h2>Section hierarchy</h2>
  <p>Structure advances from <code>h2</code> to <code>h3</code>; visual size can follow the level.</p>
  <ComponentPreview tree={headingOutlineTree(t)} label="Content hierarchy" />

  <h2>Compact hierarchy</h2>
  <p>A heading keeps its place in the document even when the visual context uses the <code>h4</code> floor.</p>
  <ComponentPreview tree={headingCompactTree(t)} label="Compact heading" />

  <h2>Flush</h2>
  <p>
    <code>data-flush</code> (React: <code>flush</code>) removes block air, top and bottom. Explicit
    opt-in: use it when the heading is first (or last) in the container (dialog title with
    <code>sk-dialog__title</code>, card header) and you do not want margin or padding pushing it
    against the edge. It is not inferred from <code>:first-child</code>.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview tree={headingFlushTree(t)} label="Heading flush" />

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={install} label="terminal" />

  <h2>React</h2>
  <p>The code is in the <strong>React</strong> tab of the preview.</p>

  <h2>Contract</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>data-size</code> accepts <code>display-lg</code>, <code>display-md</code>, <code>display-sm</code>, <code>h1</code>, <code>h2</code>, <code>h3</code>, <code>h4</code>, <code>h5</code> and <code>h6</code>. <code>h5</code>/<code>h6</code> paint the same as <code>h4</code>.</li>
    <li>Legacy aliases: <code>sm</code>→<code>h3</code>, <code>md</code>→<code>h2</code>, <code>lg</code>→<code>h1</code>, <code>display</code>→<code>display-sm</code>.</li>
    <li>In HTML, use <code>h1</code>–<code>h6</code> for document hierarchy; <code>data-size</code> does not change it.</li>
    <li>In React, <code>as</code> accepts <code>h1</code>–<code>h6</code> (default <code>h2</code>); <code>size</code> defaults to <code>h2</code>.</li>
    <li><code>data-flush</code> / React <code>flush</code>: no block margin or padding. Explicit; not automatic.</li>
    <li>Does not require vanilla initialization.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
